ting  attention so it's very typical of Tom to  do that but I'm sure glad I did it and  he's one of my greatest heroes this  film has been screened for Congress it's  been screened for the Paris mayor's  office it's been screened hugely  importantly at the Hague and in the  Italian Senate but but Jeff from the  point of view of a producer you have to  be very delicate about the POV because  if you say I'm making a film about the  sufferings of the Newman people that's  one kind of documentary but you also  don't want to say here's this white guy  who's the hero of all of this and so how  do you nuance you know selling the idea  of this and then putting together a film  that doesn't do what Tom Catina was  obviously afraid was going to happen and  I don't think it happened I think it was  so balanced and subtle well part of goes  back to when Ken first approached me  about the film the salient feature to  the story that I could tell and from a  little bit of footage that Ken showed me  was Tom Cuttino was a singular person  with a singular vocation and he had no  effect  he had no there's no pomposity to him  there was no desire to preach there was  just this guy from upstate New York who  decided to do something with his life  that was bigger than himself and it was  that altruistic aspect of him that sold  me on getting involved in the film and I  think comes through in the film so that  you just you don't see it as this great  white hope person you just see somebody  who's a human being with other human  beings  working to try to improve their lives  and you two had worked together before  so you could read each other when it  came to knowing that this was I knew a  guy who would go to Sudan and I'd be guy  who would not go you know he initially  said I don't know if that's a great idea  to do a film about a doctor in Africa  it's been done and I said let me just  sit you down and so we said wait Stanley  and Livingstone we sat down in a  Starbucks in Brentwood an