ence is also from popular  music from popular Sudanese music so in  this situation because it's a  documentary and with a very important  message the music definitely cannot get  in the way has to be understated has to  be supportive at all times there are a  few moments in the film where the music  can stand on its own where there's not  narration and then you need to have the  music say something but because of the  message and the importance of the film  the music is in a supportive way Jeff  it's hard to get financing for  documentaries and I expect the farther  you get from Los Angeles the harder it  is to get the financing but it's all a  lot of Karlsson names up there so you  know it's just this was a family  undertaking a big family support for  basically I was  fortunate enough to come on after the  money okay all right Kenwood build it so  here's an opportunity to thank again  thank all the members of your family for  each and every one thank you  it said we were blessed to be able to to  to be able to finance something like  this and it's really our passion it's my  wife Katrina I think she's out there she  she gave me a big thumbs up when she you  know heard that I wanted to do this and  as we know and documentary filmmaker  it's a labor of love and it's come back  tenfold  I'm not saying financially I'm just  saying and and what it has done and I  think we discussed this but you know the  heart of nuuma is is is primarily  responsible for the ceasefire that has  taken place we were actually able to get  the film into the hands of President  Omar Hassan al-bashir I was going to ask  if he'd seen it and so he saw it and you  know and let me just make sure I come  clean on this there are so many  organizations that are over there in  individuals that have been working  relentlessly to to expose this conflict  and to to make a difference and they  have I think we have just achieved a  tipping point so we actually were able  to get the film into into Omar Ha